WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.760
Welcome back.

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:05.677
In this class we will work through first law signs, isothermal and adiabatic

00:00:05.677 --> 00:00:08.600
derivations, path dependence and second law limits.

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:13.940
If systems A and B are each in thermal equilibrium with C, they are in thermal

00:00:13.940 --> 00:00:15.240
equilibrium with one another.

00:00:15.860 --> 00:00:19.657
This zeroth law idea makes a thermometer meaningful: it can compare

00:00:19.657 --> 00:00:20.280
temperature

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.380
after reaching equilibrium without measuring every molecule.

00:00:24.300 --> 00:00:26.020
Temperature is not the amount of heat stored.

00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:30.620
Heat names energy transferred because of a temperature difference, while

00:00:30.620 --> 00:00:32.880
internal energy is a state property of the system.

00:00:33.760 --> 00:00:35.320
Choose work by the gas as positive.

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:40.460
The first law then reads the change in internal energy equals Q minus work by

00:00:40.460 --> 00:00:40.860
the gas.

00:00:41.900 --> 00:00:46.360
Heat entering is positive Q; expansion work is usually positive work by the

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.280
gas, while compression work is negative.

00:00:49.320 --> 00:00:54.620
If 500 J enters and the gas does 200 J of work, internal energy increases by

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:55.360
300 J.

00:00:55.780 --> 00:00:59.953
The sign convention must be stated because another text may use work on the

00:00:59.953 --> 00:01:00.120
gas

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:00.560
instead.

00:01:01.460 --> 00:01:05.340
For an ideal gas with fixed composition, internal energy depends on

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.800
temperature, so equal endpoint temperatures give equal endpoint internal

00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:09.240
energies.

00:01:10.340 --> 00:01:14.120
The work done by a gas against its surroundings is the integral of external

00:01:14.120 --> 00:01:15.480
pressure with respect to volume.

00:01:16.200 --> 00:01:20.469
On a mechanically quasistatic path the gas pressure is arbitrarily close to

00:01:20.469 --> 00:01:20.640
the

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:24.580
external pressure, so the plotted equilibrium P V curve can be used.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.560
A constant pressure expansion gives the rectangular area P the volume change.

00:01:30.280 --> 00:01:34.492
An isochoric step has zero volume change and therefore zero boundary work,

00:01:34.492 --> 00:01:34.720
even

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:35.720
when pressure changes.

00:01:36.820 --> 00:01:42.800
Take endpoints A at 0.001 cubic metres and 100000 Pa, and

00:01:42.800 --> 00:01:47.700
C at 0.002 cubic metres and 200000 Pa.

00:01:47.700 --> 00:01:52.920
Expand first at 100000 Pa, then raise pressure at constant volume: work is 100

00:01:52.920 --> 00:01:53.120
J.

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:58.600
Raise pressure first, then expand at 200000 Pa: work is 200 J.

00:01:59.660 --> 00:02:03.740
The endpoints and the change in internal energy are the same, but Q differs by

00:02:03.740 --> 00:02:04.380
100 J.

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:06.560
Heat and work are path quantities.

00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:10.567
Do not use an endpoint pressure in P the volume change when the actual

00:02:10.567 --> 00:02:10.940
pressure

00:02:10.940 --> 00:02:12.000
changes along the path.

00:02:12.408 --> 00:02:16.408
For a fixed amount of ideal gas held at constant absolute temperature T,

00:02:17.068 --> 00:02:21.348
equilibrium pressure is nRT/V.

00:02:21.348 --> 00:02:24.922
Integrate this pressure from initial to final volume on a reversible

00:02:24.922 --> 00:02:25.448
isothermal

00:02:25.448 --> 00:02:25.888
path.

00:02:26.848 --> 00:02:31.308
Since nRT is constant, work by the gas is

00:02:31.308 --> 00:02:34.498
nRT

00:02:34.498 --> 00:02:37.688
ln(V_final/V_initial).

00:02:37.688 --> 00:02:41.148
Expansion has a positive logarithm; compression has a negative one.

00:02:41.528 --> 00:02:46.048
The result needs the ideal gas and reversible or quasistatic isothermal model,

00:02:46.708 --> 00:02:50.068
not just an arbitrary process whose endpoints happen to have the same

00:02:50.068 --> 00:02:50.468
temperature.

00:02:51.368 --> 00:02:56.337
Ideal gas internal energy is unchanged, so the first law gives Q equals work

00:02:56.337 --> 00:02:56.468
by

00:02:56.468 --> 00:02:57.728
the gas.

00:02:57.728 --> 00:03:01.668
Isothermal does not mean no heat transfer: heat must enter during reversible

00:03:01.668 --> 00:03:03.428
expansion to maintain temperature.

00:03:04.158 --> 00:03:09.208
For one mole at 300 K expanding from V to 2V, use R equals

00:03:09.208 --> 00:03:14.988
8.314 and ln 2 equals 0.693.

00:03:14.988 --> 00:03:18.868
Work and heat are approximately 1729 J.

00:03:18.868 --> 00:03:23.568
In contrast, ideal gas free expansion into an insulated vacuum has zero

00:03:23.568 --> 00:03:27.808
external pressure work and zero heat, but is irreversible even though its

00:03:27.808 --> 00:03:29.688
temperature remains unchanged in this model.

00:03:30.368 --> 00:03:33.588
Adiabatic means Q equals 0.

00:03:33.588 --> 00:03:39.588
For a reversible adiabatic ideal gas process, n C V dT equals P

00:03:39.588 --> 00:03:41.208
dV.

00:03:41.208 --> 00:03:45.688
Substitute P equals nRT/V and divide by T: C V

00:03:45.688 --> 00:03:50.768
dT/T equals R dV/V.

00:03:50.768 --> 00:03:54.508
If heat capacities are constant, integrate to obtain T V^(R/C V)

00:03:56.808 --> 00:03:57.168
constant.

00:03:57.908 --> 00:04:03.568
Because C P minus C V equals R and gamma equals C P/C V, the exponent

00:04:03.568 --> 00:04:04.848
is gamma minus one.

00:04:05.708 --> 00:04:09.748
Combining with the gas law gives P V^gamma constant.

00:04:10.508 --> 00:04:14.208
For a monatomic ideal gas gamma is 5/3.

00:04:14.208 --> 00:04:20.188
Doubling the volume lowers temperature to T_initial divided by 2^(2/3), so

00:04:20.648 --> 00:04:24.248
300 K becomes about 189 K.

00:04:24.248 --> 00:04:28.578
Its adiabatic work is n C V(T_initial

00:04:28.578 --> 00:04:30.448
T_final).

00:04:30.448 --> 00:04:33.108
Energy comes from internal energy, not incoming heat.

00:04:33.948 --> 00:04:38.068
Irreversible adiabatic processes need the first law and actual external work;

00:04:38.528 --> 00:04:42.208
the reversible P V^gamma relation cannot be assumed.

00:04:43.048 --> 00:04:46.968
Constant heat capacities are an additional model condition, not a promise for

00:04:46.968 --> 00:04:49.268
every real gas over every temperature range.

00:04:49.464 --> 00:04:54.784
After a complete cycle the system returns to its initial state, so net the

00:04:54.784 --> 00:04:59.804
change in internal energy is zero and net Q equals net work by the gas.

00:05:00.444 --> 00:05:06.004
On a P V diagram a clockwise quasistatic loop usually represents positive work

00:05:06.004 --> 00:05:09.584
by the gas; the enclosed signed area gives that work.

00:05:10.504 --> 00:05:16.224
For a rectangular loop between volumes 0.001 and 0.002

00:05:16.224 --> 00:05:22.084
cubic metres and pressures 100000 and 200000 Pa, clockwise work is

00:05:22.084 --> 00:05:24.204
100 J.

00:05:24.204 --> 00:05:26.044
Reversing the cycle reverses the sign.

00:05:26.964 --> 00:05:30.624
The second law prevents a cyclic engine from converting heat from a single

00:05:30.624 --> 00:05:33.824
reservoir entirely into work without other effects.

00:05:34.764 --> 00:05:39.984
A reversible Carnot engine between reservoir temperatures T_hot and T_cold has

00:05:39.984 --> 00:05:44.564
maximum efficiency 1 T_cold/T_hot, using kelvin.

00:05:45.204 --> 00:05:50.184
At 600 K and 300 K the limit is 50 percent.

00:05:50.184 --> 00:05:54.304
A real engine is less efficient under the same two reservoir conditions.

00:05:55.564 --> 00:05:59.103
Reversibility requires no entropy production and no finite gradient

00:05:59.103 --> 00:05:59.684
dissipative

00:05:59.684 --> 00:06:04.724
transfer; merely running a process slowly does not remove friction or make it

00:06:04.724 --> 00:06:05.024
reversible.
